@page "/question-types"
@using Flirty.Validation
@inject DesignerQuestionTypeSource Source
@inject FlirtyQuestionTypeRegistry Registry

<PageTitle>Question types · Flirty Designer</PageTitle>

<h1>Question types</h1>

<p class="intro">
    Host-declared custom question types (#136) that this designer knows about. They give a type its
    display name and an example answer; a question selects one by carrying its key in
    <code>CustomTypeKey</code> on the built-in type <code>Json</code>.
</p>

@* The delta is stated here as well as beside the input control, because this page is where someone
   lands who wonders why a type "does not work" - the honest answer is that the designer never runs the
   host's validation, no matter how complete this list is. *@
<p class="hint">
    <strong>What the designer does not have is the validation.</strong> An
    <code>IQuestionTypeValidator</code> is code and lives in the host process, so a test run here checks
    only that the answer is <strong>well-formed JSON</strong> — exactly what the engine checks for a
    <code>Json</code> question without a key. Declaring a type below buys a display name and a sample,
    not the host's semantic check.
</p>

<h2>Descriptor file</h2>

<p class="mono">@Source.FilePath</p>

@if (!Source.FileExists)
{
    <p class="empty">
        No descriptor file. That is not an error: without one the designer shows a custom type by its raw
        key, and everything else works unchanged.
    </p>
}

@if (Source.Problems.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="banner err">
        <p>
            <strong>@Source.Problems.Count</strong>
            @(Source.Problems.Count == 1 ? "entry was" : "entries were") skipped. The designer keeps
            running, and the remaining entries below were declared.
        </p>
        <ul>
            @foreach (var problem in Source.Problems)
            {
                <li>@problem</li>
            }
        </ul>
    </div>
}

<h2>Declared types</h2>

@if (Registry.Types.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="empty">No custom question type declared.</p>
}
else
{
    <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Key</th>
                <th>Display name</th>
                <th>Sample answer</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var type in Registry.Types)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">@type.Key</td>
                    <td>@type.DisplayName</td>
                    <td class="mono">@(type.SampleValue ?? "—")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
}

<h2>How to declare one</h2>

<p>
    Create <code>@DesignerApp.QuestionTypesFileName</code> next to
    <code>@DesignerApp.ConnectionProfilesFileName</code> in the designer's content root and restart. Keys
    use lowercase ASCII letters, digits and <code>-</code>; a sample must be valid JSON. The file is
    read once at startup and mirrors what the host passes to <code>o.AddQuestionType(...)</code>.
</p>

<pre class="mono">@ExampleFile</pre>

@code {
    private const string ExampleFile = """
        {
          "questionTypes": [
            { "key": "color", "displayName": "Colour picker", "sample": "\"#ff0000\"" },
            { "key": "address", "displayName": "Postal address",
              "sample": "{\"street\":\"Main 1\",\"zip\":\"10115\",\"city\":\"Berlin\"}" }
          ]
        }
        """;
}
